# Dependencies
node_modules/

# Vercel CLI state, wherever the project is linked.
#
# `web/.vercel/project.json` names the project id, and `web/.env.local` can hold
# an OIDC token after `vercel link`. Neither belongs in the repository, and the
# pattern is unanchored so it covers whichever directory gets linked next --
# dashboard/ has the same pair today.
.vercel/
.env*
!.env.example

# Next.js build output.
#
# This directory is generated by `npm run build` and copied into the Go binary
# at compile time. It is not committed: the binary is the artifact, and
# checking in generated HTML would put two sources of truth for the dashboard
# in the repository.
#
# `internal/server/assets.go` embeds `assets/`, not this path, so the Go build
# only ever depends on the copy step.
dashboard/out/
dashboard/.next/
dashboard/next-env.d.ts
dashboard/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# The vendored copy that actually gets embedded.
#
# `.gitkeep` IS committed: the `//go:embed all:assets` directive needs at least
# one file in the directory for `go build` to succeed on a clean checkout. The
# server detects the absence of a real index.html and serves an honest
# placeholder instead of a blank page.
internal/server/assets/*
!internal/server/assets/.gitkeep

# Local analysis database.
.lensyxe/

# Launch-video render output.
#
# `brag-output/` is generated: a render plan, an mp4, and the Hyperframes
# project that produced it. None of it is source, and the mp4 in particular is a
# few megabytes of binary that would change on every re-render. Anchored to the
# repository root for the same reason as the binaries below -- an unanchored
# pattern would match a directory of that name at any depth.
brag-output/
# Every later run lands in a timestamped directory (brag-output-YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS)
# so an earlier run is never overwritten. That naming is load-bearing rather than
# cosmetic: a timestamped name matches this glob and is excluded by the same rule,
# so without it each extra run appears as untracked noise in `git status` and a
# multi-megabyte mp4 could be committed by accident.
brag-output-*/

# Build output.
#
# The leading slash is load-bearing. A bare `lensyxe` pattern has no slash, so
# git treats it as matching a file OR DIRECTORY of that name at ANY depth, which
# silently ignored the entire cmd/lensyxe/ directory. The repository committed
# every internal package and every test, and none of the actual program. Anchor
# both patterns to the repository root so they match only a binary built in the
# working directory.
/lensyxe
/lensyxe.exe

# Any other executable anywhere in the tree.
*.exe
!action/*.exe